---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const enCfg = {
  points: [{
    points: [[1, 2.20], [3, 0.98], [4, 1.57], [5, 2.04], [6, 2.55], [7, 3.04], [8, 3.44], [9, 3.98], [11, 0.93], [12, 1.31], [13, 1.61], [14, 1.90], [15, 2.19], [16, 2.58], [17, 3.16], [19, 0.82], [20, 1.00]],
    label: 'electronegativity (Pauling)', mode: 'both', color: '#d9480f',
  }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 21, label: 'atomic number Z' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 4.5 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const readCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'a period = a filling shell', note: 'Moving left → right across a row, electrons fill the same outer shell (2, then 8, then 8…).' },
    { math: 'a group = the same outer count', note: 'Down a column, every element has the same number of outer (valence) electrons.' },
    { math: 'same valence → same chemistry', note: 'All alkali metals (group 1) explode in water; all noble gases (group 18) react with almost nothing.' },
    { math: 'Mendeleev left gaps — and won', note: 'In 1869 he left blank spaces and predicted missing elements. Germanium, found in 1886, matched his "eka-silicon" almost exactly.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/periodic-table">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the 63 known elements by atomic weight and noticed the
    properties <em>repeated</em> — periodically. Instead of forcing the pattern, he left gaps for
    undiscovered elements and predicted their properties. When gallium (1875), scandium (1879)
    and germanium (1886) turned up on schedule, the table became chemistry's foundation. Today it
    runs to <strong>118 elements</strong> (the last four named in 2016), ordered by proton count
    rather than weight.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="electronegativity across the first 20 elements — the saw-tooth resets at every new shell" widget="plotter" config={enCfg} />

  <p>
    The zig-zag <em>is</em> the table's logic: electronegativity (an atom's pull on electrons)
    climbs across each period toward the halogens, then crashes back at the alkali metals where a
    new shell starts. Fluorine, at 3.98, is the hungriest element of all.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="how to read the table in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={readCfg} />

  <h2>Why it looks like that</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rows (periods) 1–7</strong> correspond to electron shells; the table's width is forced by how many fit per shell — 2, 8, 8, 18…</li>
    <li><strong>Columns (groups) share valence electrons</strong>, so they share chemistry: group 1 are soft reactive metals, group 17 are hungry non-metals, group 18 are aloof gases.</li>
    <li><strong>The staircase line</strong> separates metals (left, ~75% of the table) from non-metals; elements astride it (silicon, germanium) are the semiconductors your phone runs on.</li>
    <li><strong>Element 118, oganesson</strong>, exists for milliseconds at a time — the table's edge is still being pushed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mendeleev (1869); IUPAC periodic table (118 elements); Pauling electronegativity scale.</p>
</TopicPage>
